  designed-in part of this, not a later cleanup — I'll build that into the design rather than treat it as optional.

  First question, and it forks the whole design:

  The repo contains two unrelated programs — a browser app ([38;5;153mindex.html[39m + [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, the login form) and a Node CLI
  ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m). They share no code and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m points [38;5;153mmain[39m at the Node side. Where the logs need
  to end up differs completely between them: browser logs have to be shipped over the network to be readable in
  production at all (transport, batching, failure handling, user privacy), while Node logs can go to stdout and be
  picked up by whatever runs the process (no transport, structured JSON, far cheaper). Picking both means one logger
  abstraction with two backends — more design, and the browser constraints dominate.

  - [1mBrowser app only[0m — buys real visibility into the thing that has actual users and failures; costs a log-shipping
    decision (endpoint, batching, what happens offline) and a hard privacy story around the login form.
  - [1mNode CLI only[0m — cheapest by a wide margin: structured lines to stdout, no transport, no privacy exposure. But
    [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a [38;5;153mgreet('world')[39m stub, so there's little to debug.
  - [1mBoth, shared logger[0m — one API across the two, worth it only if these programs are meant to converge; otherwise it's
    an abstraction serving one real consumer.

  Which is expensive to change later: the transport choice and the log record shape (field names, level set), since
  anything consuming the logs depends on them. Which is cheap: log levels at individual call sites, and what's logged
  where.

  My recommendation is the browser app only. It's the code with real production failure modes, and scoping to it keeps
  the privacy work focused where the sensitive data actually is.
